{"candidates":[{"content":"SRC_005 reduces to SRC_002: the pruning-via-efficiency claim depends on the assumption that the lineage-dependency tax is the price of high-confidence source facts, but this is exactly the suppression mechanism restated as a virtue. The reduction is REFUTED IF an ownership-allocation regime is observed to produce cross-cutting explanations at or above the rate of a random-allocation regime, since that would break the equivalence between pruning and suppression.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"4a5945402e166090bad490ac888879881ec732a9234a8accbe6347eb1e9d229b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"04d78b513f105fe9069cb5f91a82b7ba49b87c15eb7bd0d30f7990af8ca5c2c8"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"SRC_005 contradicts SRC_004 because they share the mechanism of lineage-local basin descent but reach opposite conclusions about its effect: SRC_005 claims efficiency is a precondition for cross-cutting discovery, while SRC_004 claims that same efficiency is the artifact of suppression. The contradiction is REFUTED IF the efficiency gains of ownership allocation are empirically separable from suppression—i.e., if remaining within a single basin can be shown to accelerate saddle-crossing rather than prevent it.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"4a5945402e166090bad490ac888879881ec732a9234a8accbe6347eb1e9d229b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3fcbaa426947faeba5fb64c80010bfae5825ed749d9b2720642d7426b7e89957"}],"typicality":0.5},{"content":"SRC_006 abstracts SRC_004: the optimization-basin saddle-crossing model is an instance of the broader institutional-constraint rational-optimization pattern, where the agent's optimization metric (loss landscape) is a concrete realization of the institutional constraint (ownership lineage). This abstraction is REFUTED IF the shared structure breaks down because research-programme landscapes are co-constructed by the frames used to search them, making the fixed-landscape assumption of gradient descent a substantive disanalogy rather than a mere implementation detail.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"099c8bad17799ea402707aeb0c388d79f49727fab698077e0e7d645bb6addf97"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3fcbaa426947faeba5fb64c80010bfae5825ed749d9b2720642d7426b7e89957"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"SRC_004 depends on SRC_002: the directed-suppression mechanism (over-suppression of low-energy saddle-crossing) inherits the lineage-dependency tax, because without the tax there is no cost asymmetry between within-basin descent and cross-basin exploration, and thus no directed bias. This dependence is REFUTED IF the saddle-crossing costs are intrinsic to the problem space and independent of the allocation regime—i.e., if rival lineages face identical saddle heights regardless of ownership.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"3fcbaa426947faeba5fb64c80010bfae5825ed749d9b2720642d7426b7e89957"},{"role":"dependence","target":"04d78b513f105fe9069cb5f91a82b7ba49b87c15eb7bd0d30f7990af8ca5c2c8"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"SRC_005 inherits SRC_003: the pruning-via-efficiency defense inherits the structural necessity of ownership-based localization for fact consolidation, since without ownership the invariants that make source-programme facts high-confidence would not carry forward. This inheritance is REFUTED IF a non-ownership allocation regime (e.g., open cross-lineage borrowing) is empirically observed to produce equally or more reliable consolidated facts, which would mean the inheritance is incidental rather than structural.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"4a5945402e166090bad490ac888879881ec732a9234a8accbe6347eb1e9d229b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"d25e25f262a80faf92493ce071b20d61c31235e7b28d440bcdf9597907fefa42"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"SRC_006 contradicts SRC_003 because the shared institutional-constraint structure predicts systematic underrepresentation of best-